    body: `Among the five facts that make up the historian's minimal-facts case for the resurrection, the empty tomb occupies a peculiar position: it is the one piece of evidence that the earliest opponents of Christianity did not attempt to deny. The oldest recorded Jewish response to the resurrection proclamation, preserved in Matthew's Gospel and echoed decades later in Justin Martyr's Dialogue with Trypho, was not "the tomb was never empty" but "the disciples stole the body." That response only makes sense as damage control for a fact both sides already agreed on. If the tomb had still contained a body, the authorities in Jerusalem — who had every motive and every means to end the fledgling movement — could simply have walked a short distance, produced the corpse, and the resurrection proclamation would have died on the spot, in the very city where it began.

The tomb tradition is also attested with striking independence: it appears in Mark, the earliest Gospel; in the pre-Pauline creed embedded in 1 Corinthians 15 ("he was buried, and he was raised"), which explicitly presupposes that what was buried is what rose; and again in Matthew, Luke, and John, each drawing on partially independent sources. Perhaps the single strongest internal evidence for its historicity is a detail that no one inventing the story would have chosen to include: all four Gospels report women as the first witnesses to the empty tomb. In first-century Jewish and Greco-Roman courts, women's testimony was not considered legally reliable — a detail Josephus himself confirms. A fabricated legend, crafted to be persuasive, would have named male witnesses as the discoverers; the Gospels' willingness to preserve an embarrassing detail that actually weakened their case in the eyes of contemporaries is difficult to explain except as an unwelcome fact the writers felt bound to report because it happened that way.

The two most common naturalistic alternatives — that the women mistakenly visited the wrong tomb, or that someone moved or stole the body — fail to account for the full range of evidence together. The wrong-tomb theory does not explain why the authorities, who certainly knew which tomb was Jesus's, never simply corrected the mistake publicly; and any theft or removal of the body still leaves the disciples' subsequent experiences of a risen Jesus, and their willingness to suffer and die proclaiming it, entirely unexplained. The empty tomb, combined with the appearances and the disciples' transformation, is why even many scholars who reject the resurrection as a historical event nonetheless accept the emptiness of the tomb itself as among the best-established facts in the entire episode.`,
