    body: `N.T. Wright frames the historical puzzle behind the resurrection not merely in terms of individual facts but in terms of a "mutation" that requires explanation: something happened that caused a small group of defeated, frightened Galilean fishermen to proclaim, within weeks and in the very city where their leader had just been publicly executed as a criminal, that he had bodily risen from the dead. Sociologist of religion Rodney Stark's quantitative analysis in The Rise of Christianity traced the movement's growth from perhaps a thousand believers around the year 40 to some six million by the year 300 — a sustained growth rate of roughly 40% per decade, achieved without military conquest, without state patronage, and, for most of that period, in the teeth of active persecution rather than social advantage.

What makes this growth curve historically puzzling, rather than merely impressive, is where and how it began. The explosion of the movement occurred first in Jerusalem itself, within weeks of the crucifixion — precisely the place where the evidence needed to disprove the resurrection claim, if it were false, was most immediately available. The tomb was a short walk away. Witnesses to the crucifixion, the burial, and the alleged appearances were still alive and present in the city. The religious and civic authorities who had colluded to have Jesus executed had every motive to stamp out the movement in its cradle, and the means to do so were, in principle, simple: produce the body, or demonstrate that no empty tomb existed. That the movement instead took root and grew explosively in exactly this hostile environment, among people who had the most access to potential counter-evidence, is what historian Larry Hurtado's work on the sociological peculiarity of early Christianity emphasizes: this movement does not fit the pattern of successful religious movements that spread through political backing or philosophical prestige, both of which early Christianity conspicuously lacked.

Comparisons to other rapidly growing religious movements do not hold up under scrutiny. Islam's early expansion was substantially advanced through military conquest and state sponsorship; Buddhism's spread across Asia was aided by royal patronage in several kingdoms. Early Christianity, by contrast, spread through a message centered on a criminal's shameful execution, offering its adherents nothing but social marginalization and, frequently, death — and yet it took hold first and fastest exactly where its central claim was most easily falsifiable. The argument does not by itself prove the resurrection; but it does place a heavy burden on any purely naturalistic account of Christian origins to explain why a movement with every apparent disadvantage succeeded precisely where and when its founding claim was most vulnerable to disproof.`,
