    body: `The argument from fulfilled prophecy carries an evidential weight that most historical arguments for Christianity lack: it can, in principle, be checked against physical texts whose dates of composition are independently verifiable, rather than resting solely on the interpretation of later testimony. The discovery of the Dead Sea Scrolls between 1947 and 1956 settled what had once been a live dispute — scrolls containing the Book of Isaiah and the Psalms, dated by paleography and radiocarbon analysis to between roughly 250 and 68 BC, prove conclusively that the prophetic texts Christians point to already existed, word for word, at least two centuries before Jesus was born. Whatever explains the correspondence between these texts and the events of the Gospels, it cannot be that Jewish scribes wrote the prophecies after the fact to match a life already lived.

The specificity of the alleged correspondences is what gives the argument its force. Micah, writing centuries earlier, named Bethlehem as the birthplace of Israel's coming ruler. Zechariah described a king entering Jerusalem mounted on a donkey, and, in a separate passage, described a betrayal for the specific sum of thirty pieces of silver. Psalm 22 describes, in poetic language predating Roman crucifixion practice in the region by centuries, a sufferer whose hands and feet are pierced and whose garments are divided by casting lots, while Psalm 34 specifies that none of the sufferer's bones would be broken — a detail John's Gospel notes was fulfilled precisely because the soldiers, breaking the legs of the two men crucified beside Jesus to hasten death, found him already dead and left his bones intact. Isaiah 53, the most theologically dense of these texts, describes a servant who suffers and is "cut off" for the transgressions of others and yet, in its concluding verses, is somehow vindicated afterward — language the Dead Sea Scrolls confirm existed in essentially its current form well before the Christian era, and which some strands of pre-Christian Jewish interpretation had already begun to read messianically.

What gives the argument additional force is that many of the details fulfilled were entirely beyond Jesus's own control to orchestrate even if he had wished to stage them: he could not choose his own birthplace, could not compel Roman soldiers to cast lots for his clothing or to leave his legs unbroken, and could not engineer Judas's decision to betray him for a specific sum of silver decades before he was born. Critics have argued that the Gospel writers may have shaped their narratives to echo these texts deliberately, and in a few explicit citations this is acknowledged even by defenders of the argument; but the earliest Christian testimony — the creedal formula in 1 Corinthians 15, predating any of the Gospels — already asserts that Christ died and rose "according to the scriptures," suggesting the conviction that Jesus fulfilled prophecy was present from the very beginning of the Christian movement, not a literary embellishment added by later editors.`,
