    body: `Every historical argument for the resurrection or the truth of Christianity presupposes something more basic that is worth stating explicitly and defending on its own terms: that Jesus of Nazareth actually existed as a flesh-and-blood historical figure. Fringe "mythicist" theories, popular in some corners of the internet, hold that Jesus was a purely legendary or mythological construction with no historical basis at all — a position so far outside mainstream scholarly consensus that it is rejected by essentially every professional historian who has studied the question, including scholars who are personally hostile to Christian belief.

The most significant witness to this consensus is Bart Ehrman, an agnostic New Testament scholar and one of the most prominent public critics of Christian apologetics writing today, who nonetheless wrote an entire book, Did Jesus Exist?, for the specific purpose of refuting mythicism from a secular, critical standpoint. His argument rests on independent, multiply attested sources that meet ordinary historical standards regardless of anyone's theological commitments: Paul's letters, whose authenticity is essentially undisputed among critical scholars, describe Paul personally meeting "James, the Lord's brother" in Jerusalem within a few years of the crucifixion — a casual, incidental reference to a real family relationship, not a theological argument. The Jewish historian Josephus, writing toward the end of the first century, refers in an uncontested passage to "James, the brother of Jesus who is called Christ," confirming both men's existence as a matter of routine historical record-keeping, with no apologetic motive whatsoever. The Roman historian Tacitus, writing around 116 AD with evident distaste for the Christian movement, records that "Christus... suffered the extreme penalty during the reign of Tiberius at the hands of one of our procurators, Pontius Pilatus" — testimony from a source who had every reason to get the basic facts right and none to invent them.

A separate, more contested passage in Josephus, the so-called Testimonium Flavianum, is widely regarded by scholars as a genuine reference to Jesus that was later embellished by Christian copyists — the scholarly consensus holds that a real, more modest original statement underlies the surviving text, rather than the whole passage being a later fabrication. Taken together, this body of independent testimony — Jewish, Roman, and Christian, some of it hostile, none of it interested in supporting later Christian claims about the resurrection — establishes Jesus's existence and crucifixion under Pontius Pilate about as securely as any event from the ancient world can be established. This is precisely why serious historical debate about Jesus, even among skeptical scholars, has moved past the question of whether he existed and focused instead on the far more consequential question of who he was and what happened after his death — the question every other argument in this section takes up in turn.`,
